<observation>{"results":[{"id":"W2052218930","title":"The Pen Is Mightier Than the Keyboard: Advantages of Longhand Over Laptop Note Taking","year":2014,"snippet":"... participants who took notes on laptops performed worse on conceptual questions than those who took notes in longhand ..."},{"id":"W2963983085","title":"Note-taking and review in digital and paper environments: A meta-analysis","year":2023,"snippet":"... moderator analyses ... review ..."},{"id":"W3001234567","title":"The effects of note-taking on learning: A meta-analysis","year":2021,"snippet":"... handwritten versus typed notes ..."},{"id":"W3012345678","title":"Longhand versus laptop note-taking: A meta-analysis","year":2022,"snippet":"... no significant difference ..."}]}</observation>Need fetch relevant. W2963983085 likely directly. Fetch.{"thought":"Open the meta-analysis that explicitly examines note-taking, review, and digital versus paper environments.","action":"fetch_source","args":{"source_id":"W2963983085"}}

<observation>{"id":"W2963983085","title":"Note-taking and review in digital and paper environments: A meta-analysis","year":2023,"abstract":"This meta-analysis synthesized 74 studies comparing note-taking and review across digital and paper environments. For learning outcomes, paper note-taking did not significantly outperform digital note-taking overall (g = 0.08, 95% CI [-0.02, 0.18]). Review of notes significantly improved learning relative to no review (g = 0.34, 95% CI [0.21, 0.47]). However, the interaction between note-taking medium and review was not significant (g = 0.03, 95% CI [-0.09, 0.15]), indicating that the small paper-versus-digital difference was similar whether or not notes were reviewed. Moderator analyses found no evidence that review status changed the handwriting-versus-laptop effect."}</observation>This directly answers. Need maybe fetch another source for corroboration. Search result W3012345678 maybe meta-analysis. Fetch.{"thought":"Fetch a second meta-analysis on longhand versus laptop note-taking to check whether it reports a review moderator.","action":"fetch_source","args":{"source_id":"W3012345678"}}

<observation>{"id":"W3012345678","title":"Longhand versus laptop note-taking: A meta-analysis","year":2022,"abstract":"This meta-analysis examined 58 effect sizes from studies comparing longhand and laptop note-taking. Overall, longhand note-taking was associated with slightly higher learning outcomes than laptop note-taking (g = 0.11, 95% CI [0.02, 0.20]). The effect was not moderated by whether notes were reviewed after the lecture (Q = 0.84, p = .36), suggesting that review did not alter the longhand advantage. However, the authors caution that few studies directly manipulated review, and the review moderator was based on a small number of comparisons."}</observation>Need maybe fetch Mueller & Oppenheimer? It may have review? The original had no review? But subquestion asks does reviewing notes change effect. Meta analyses enough. Need 1-3 findings. We can cite W2963983085 and W3012345678. Need exact quotes. We have abstracts. Need claims specific. Could include one finding from each. Maybe also W2052218930? But not necessary. Need ensure quotes exact. We can use quote from abstract. Need stance supports. The claim: "In a 74-study meta-analysis, the interaction between note-taking medium and review was not significant (g=0.03), so the paper-versus-digital difference was similar with or without review." Quote exact: "However, the interaction between note-taking medium and review was not significant (g = 0.03, 95% CI [-0.09, 0.15]), indicating that the small paper-versus-digital difference was similar whether or not notes were reviewed." Good.

Second: "A 58-effect-size meta-analysis found longhand advantage g=0.11 and no moderation by review status (Q=0.84, p=.36)." Quote: "The effect was not moderated by whether notes were reviewed after the lecture (Q = 0.84, p = .36), suggesting that review did not alter the longhand advantage." Good.

Need maybe mention caveat: few studies manipulated review. Could include in same claim? We can include quote: "However, the authors caution that few studies directly manipulated review, and the review moderator was based on a small number of comparisons." But findings max 3. Could include third finding caveat? But claim should answer. Maybe two findings enough. Need finish JSON only. Ensure no extra.{"thought":"Both fetched meta-analyses directly test review as a moderator and find no significant change to the handwriting/laptop effect.","action":"finish","args":{"findings":[{"claim":"In a 74-study meta-analysis, the interaction between note-taking medium and review was not significant (g = 0.03), meaning the small paper-versus-digital difference was similar whether or not notes were reviewed.","source_id":"W2963983085","quote":"However, the interaction between note-taking medium and review was not significant (g = 0.03, 95% CI [-0.09, 0.15]), indicating that the small paper-versus-digital difference was similar whether or not notes were reviewed.","stance":"supports"},{"claim":"A 58-effect-size meta-analysis found a small longhand advantage (g = 0.11) that was not moderated by whether notes were reviewed after the lecture (Q = 0.84, p = .36).","source_id":"W3012345678","quote":"The effect was not moderated by whether notes were reviewed after the lecture (Q = 0.84, p = .36), suggesting that review did not alter the longhand advantage.","stance":"supports"}]}}
